3. IMAGE INCONSISTENCIES (CAUSE OF "ALL IMAGES NOT SAME")
================================================================================

3a. Image sizing strategies differ per component:

  built-img (landing.css:246):         width: 100% (no max-width, no MQ)
  work-with-img (landing.css:250):     width: 100% (no max-width, no MQ)
  help-img (landing.css:254):          width: 100%, max-width: 497px
  global-img (landing.css:259):        width: 100% (no max-width, no MQ)
  candidate-img (candidate-card.css):  width: 100%, height: 190px, object-fit: scale-down
  professional-img (about.css:67):     max-width: 434px (no width: 100% fallback!)
  what-makes-img (about.css:99,105):   max-width: 100% (mobile) / max-width: 554px (desktop)
  org-built-img (org.css:49):          max-width: 100% (no fixed width)
  support-img (job-seekers.css:49):    max-width: 100% (on mobile: max-width: 54%, margin: auto)
  dollar-img (globals.css:135):        width+height change per breakpoint (granular MQ)
  footer-logo-img (footer.css:139):    width+height fixed, changes per MQ

3b. Missing responsive behavior for images:
  - built-img, work-with-img, global-img: NO media queries at all
  - help-img: only has max-width: 497px, no mobile override
  - professional-img: only has max-width: 434px -> 300px on mobile (no width:100% fallback)

3c. Mix of `<img>` and CSS background-image approaches:
  - Content images: Use Next.js `<Image>` with className + CSS sizing
  - Hero images: ALL use CSS `background-image` (17 hero sections)
  
3d. Image aspect ratio not preserved consistently:
  - candidate-img uses `object-fit: scale-down` with fixed height 190px (can letterbox)
  - Section images (built-img etc.) use `width: 100%` without height — relies on intrinsic
  - what-makes-img has no height at all (relies on Next.js Image aspect ratio)
  - support-img has `margin: auto` + `max-width: 54%` on mobile (very different approach)

ROOT CAUSE: No shared image component or consistent sizing strategy. Each 
section defines its own image sizing rules independently.

